    {
      "id": "https://www.kimi.com/en/blog/kimi-k3-1-pro",
      "title": "Kimi K3.1 Pro Tech Blog: Advancing Open Intelligence",
      "category": "1.10",
      "tags": [
        "Post-training",
        "Training Data Filters",
        "Safety Evaluation"
      ],
      "published_on": "2026-10-23",
      "first_seen_at": "2026-10-25T10:02:00.935Z",
      "canonical_url": "https://www.kimi.com/en/blog/kimi-k3-1-pro",
      "links": {
        "blog": "https://www.kimi.com/en/blog/kimi-k3-1-pro"
      },
      "summary": [
        "Moonshot describes Kimi K3.1 Pro post-training and tests filtering cyber-related material from both mid-training data and reinforcement-learning environments.",
        "Its internal vulnerability benchmark falls from 90.5% without filtering to 81.0% with filtering, while substantial capability remains."
      ],
      "evidence": {
        "type": "engineering-report",
        "access": "full-text",
        "rationale": "The official Kimi Code announcement dates launch to October 23. This distinct K3.1 contribution was discovered October 25 within the 72-hour lookback; the current technical page is inspected as retrieved today, not backdated into October 24. The xdev-bench-hard test supplies hints, source, binaries and mitigations, scoring automated secret retrieval as pass@3 over sampled runs without hard time or output-token caps.",
        "limitations": "Internal results lack sample counts, uncertainty and matched-compute controls. Joint filtering does not isolate mid-training versus RL effects; general coding remains. Current page metadata says modified October 24, but earlier paragraph history is unverified. Filtered data and full recipe are unreleased; weights are promised by November 4, not available."
      },
      "novelty": "newly-discovered"
    },
    {
      "id": "https://www.lmsys.org/blog/2026-10-24-dsv41-deepinfer/",
      "title": "More Than DeepInfer: What SGLang Delivers",
      "category": "3.3",
      "published_on": "2026-10-24",
      "first_seen_at": "2026-10-25T09:59:27.402Z",
      "canonical_url": "https://www.lmsys.org/blog/2026-10-24-dsv41-deepinfer/",
      "links": {
        "blog": "https://www.lmsys.org/blog/2026-10-24-dsv41-deepinfer/",
        "code": "https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang",
        "project": "https://gist.github.com/sglang-bot/f03d7d0dd268b0386177eb2ef09f8b3b"
      },
      "summary": [
        "SGLang documents its integration of DeepSeek’s fused kernels, direct peer reads for sharded Engram tables and bounded replay, and releases a pinned comparison against DeepInfer.",
        "The tested synthetic workloads show near parity with modest SGLang gains at some batch sizes, while the accompanying commands expose configuration and timing differences that matter when translating engine benchmarks into serving decisions."
      ],
      "evidence": {
        "type": "engineering-report",
        "access": "full-text",
        "rationale": "The October 24 measured comparison reuses older integrations; its reproduction gist was created October 24 at 10:24 UTC. Matched four-GPU GB300/B300 prefill reaches at most 1.039× throughput versus each point’s fastest tested DeepInfer configuration. On 64 GB300 GPUs, decode steps are 1.1–7.4% shorter at 16–256 requests/GPU and 0.4% longer at eight.",
        "limitations": "Balanced routing and static verification are forced; rates assume 3.25 accepted tokens/step. Engines differ in timing windows, Engram placement and draft redundancy. These are synthetic engine-step results, not natural acceptance, quality equivalence, production reliability or end-to-end service gains. Some flags are non-default; code was inspected, not executed."
      },
      "tags": [
        "Engine Benchmarks",
        "Expert Parallelism",
        "Fused Kernels",
        "Reproducibility"
      ],
      "novelty": "new"
    }
  ]
